window.HELP_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"PraiseAV Help","heading":"","text":"PraiseAV Help Welcome. PraiseAV puts songs, Bible readings, videos and sermons on your church’s screens. It’s free, and it’s made to be simple enough for any volunteer on a Sunday morning.","summary":"Start here: guides for your first Sunday, every feature, example setups and hardware."},{"url":"index.html#new-to-praiseav","page":"PraiseAV Help","heading":"New to PraiseAV?","text":"New to PraiseAV? Start with Your first Sunday . It takes you from installing PraiseAV to running a whole service, one step at a time. No experience needed. Used EasyWorship before? Coming from EasyWorship shows where everything is, and how to bring your songs and backgrounds across.","summary":""},{"url":"index.html#find-what-you-need","page":"PraiseAV Help","heading":"Find what you need","text":"Find what you need Type in the search box at the top of any page: try “projector”, “alert” or “NIV”. Or pick a topic: I want to… Read Add songs and change their words Songs Show a Bible reading, including the NIV The Bible Build a service and save it to take to church Services Print a service sheet for the congregation Printing Run the service on Sunday Running a service Give the preacher their notes and a timer Sermons and the preacher’s screen Call a parent from the crèche Parent alerts Use moving backgrounds, videos and music Media and Music and audio Show a guest speaker’s PowerPoint PowerPoint and PDF Set up the projector and other screens Screens Livestream the service Livestream and ATEM Record the service to upload later Recording the service Use old phones as cameras Live video Show PraiseAV to another church The demo church Work PraiseAV from my phone Phone remote Run a service with no one at the laptop Without an AV volunteer See what equipment other churches use Example setups and Hardware Fix something that isn’t working Troubleshooting Tip: In PraiseAV, click ? Help at the top right, or press F1 at any time to open the help about what you’re doing.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"","text":"Your first Sunday This guide takes you from nothing to a whole service on the screen. Take it slowly; you can’t break anything. Allow about half an hour the first time, ideally a day or two before Sunday. You’ll need: a Windows computer or laptop (Windows 10 or 11) your projector or TV, and the cable to connect it (usually HDMI ) if your church used EasyWorship before, the computer it was on","summary":"The beginner's guide: install PraiseAV, plug in the projector, build a service and run it, step by step."},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-1-install-praiseav","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 1: Install PraiseAV","text":"Step 1: Install PraiseAV Install PraiseAV from the Microsoft Store (search for “PraiseAV”), or from praiseav.com . Open it from the Start menu. The first time it opens, PraiseAV says hello and asks a few questions. You can come back to them any time with Getting started… at the bottom right of the window. Used EasyWorship? Choose Yes , and PraiseAV brings your songs and backgrounds across. EasyWorship itself isn’t changed. Starting fresh? Choose No . PraiseAV comes with some well-known hymns to start you off. Then type your church’s name and choose your logo (a picture file). The logo is what the screen shows when you press Logo .","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-2-plug-in-the-projector","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 2: Plug in the projector","text":"Step 2: Plug in the projector Connect the projector (or TV) to the computer with its HDMI cable, and switch it on. Press the Windows key and P together, and choose Extend . PraiseAV puts the words on the projector by itself. Important: It must be Extend , not Duplicate . With Duplicate , the projector just copies your screen, and the congregation would see everything you do. Check: the top of PraiseAV shows a green dot and Congregation . That means the projector is ready. If you’re unsure which screen is which, open Screens… and click Identify screens : each screen shows a big number. See Screens for more.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-3-find-your-way-around","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 3: Find your way around","text":"Step 3: Find your way around The window has four columns: Library (left): your songs, the Bible, media (pictures, videos, music) and themes. Service (second): the order of today’s service, top to bottom. Slides (middle): the slides of whatever you’ve picked in the service. On screen (right): exactly what the congregation can see now, and what comes next.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-4-build-the-service","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 4: Build the service","text":"Step 4: Build the service Add songs. In the Songs tab, type a few words of the title or the words into the search box, then double-click the song. It goes into the service. Add a Bible reading. Open the Bible tab, type the reference (short forms are fine, like ps 23 or jn 3 16-18 ), then press Enter . Add the sermon. Click + Sermon at the top of the Service column. Type the title and the preacher’s name. If they’ve sent notes, paste them in; they’ll appear only on the preacher’s screen. See Sermons . Change the order. Drag items up and down the service. To take one out, point at it and click the ✕ . Give it a name and save it. Type a name at the top of the Service column, like “Sunday 18 October”, and click Save . See Services to keep services on OneDrive or a USB stick.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-5-practise","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 5: Practise","text":"Step 5: Practise Before Sunday, run through the service once, even on your own at home: Click the first item in the service, then click its first slide. It appears on the screen, and the slide has a red border in PraiseAV. Press Next (or the → key) to move on. At the end of a song, Next carries on into the next item. Try Black , Clear text and Logo , and press them again to come back. No projector at home? Open Screens… and click Open practice screens to see what every screen would show. See Screens .","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-6-on-sunday","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 6: On Sunday","text":"Step 6: On Sunday Switch on the projector and computer early, and open PraiseAV. It remembers the service you were working on. Before the service, show your Logo (press L ) or your notices . When the first song starts, click its first slide, then press Next at the end of each verse. Watch Up next on the right. It shows exactly what Next will put on the screen, so there are no surprises. Between items, press Logo or Black . Tip: A presentation clicker (the kind used for PowerPoint) works with PraiseAV straight away: forward is Next, back is Previous, and the blank button is Black.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#if-something-goes-wrong","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"If something goes wrong","text":"If something goes wrong The words aren’t on the projector: if there’s a yellow message at the top, click Use it as a separate screen . Otherwise check Screens… . Wrong slide on screen: click the right one; it changes straight away. Need a moment: press Black . Nothing is lost. More in Troubleshooting . In PraiseAV, click ? Help at the top right (or press F1 ) to come back to this guide at any time. Well done: you’re ready for Sunday.","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"","text":"Coming from EasyWorship PraiseAV works the way EasyWorship users expect, so most things will feel familiar. This page shows how to bring your things across and where to find everything.","summary":"Bring your EasyWorship songs, schedules and backgrounds across, and find where everything is in PraiseAV."},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#bring-your-songs-and-backgrounds-across","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"Bring your songs and backgrounds across","text":"Bring your songs and backgrounds across Do this on the computer that has EasyWorship on it. In PraiseAV, click Getting started… (bottom right), then Yes: bring my songs and backgrounds across . PraiseAV finds EasyWorship and shows how many songs and backgrounds it found. Click Bring in the songs , then Bring them in for the backgrounds, pictures and videos. Songs you already have are skipped, so it’s safe to do again. Backgrounds go into the Media tab, under Backgrounds › From EasyWorship . EasyWorship itself isn’t changed or removed. You can keep using it while you try PraiseAV. Videos in old formats (WMV, AVI, MPEG) can’t be played. The free HandBrake app turns them into MP4, which works. You can also bring songs in from the Songs tab at any time: click From EasyWorship .","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#easyworship-on-a-different-computer","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"EasyWorship on a different computer","text":"EasyWorship on a different computer Copy EasyWorship’s song library (the files Songs.db and SongWords.db , usually in Public Documents › Softouch › EasyWorship › Default › Databases › Data ) onto a USB stick. Then, in PraiseAV, use From EasyWorship and choose Songs.db from the stick.","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#easyworship-schedules","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"EasyWorship schedules","text":"EasyWorship schedules Saved schedules ( .ewsx files) can be imported with Import in the Songs tab. Their songs and Bible readings are added to the end of the service. Videos and presentations in a schedule aren’t brought across: add them again from the Media tab.","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#where-things-are","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"Where things are","text":"Where things are In EasyWorship In PraiseAV Schedule The Service column. Double-click songs to add them, or use + Sermon and + Slides . Preview and Live Click a slide to put it on screen. Up next (right) shows exactly what Next will show. Go to the next slide (Page Down, clicker) Next , the → key, Space or Page Down . Clickers work as before. Black, Clear, Logo The same buttons, with the keys B , C and L . Themes The Themes tab. Choose a theme for songs, Bible and sermons, or for one item. See Themes . Media (backgrounds, videos, images) The Media tab. Use as background puts a moving background behind the words. Audio Music goes in the Media tab too, and plays from Music and audio on the right. See Music and audio . PowerPoint + Add PowerPoint or PDF in the Media tab. PowerPoint doesn’t need to be installed. See PowerPoint and PDF . Presentations + Slides opens the slide designer . Foldback (stage display) The Preacher screen: notes, the next slide, the clock, the timer and prompts. See Sermons . Alternate output The Livestream screen: the words in a strip for your ATEM or video mixer. See Livestream . Nursery alerts Parent alerts , with your own names for the groups. See Parent alerts . Countdowns Timer and prompts on the right. See Timers . Announcement loops Set any slides to Loop by itself . See Announcement loops . Saving and transferring schedules Save and Open , on this computer, OneDrive, Google Drive or a USB stick. See Services . Bible (NIV) NIV and more from bible.com in the Bible tab. See The Bible . SongSelect Download the chord chart or lyrics file from SongSelect, then Import it. See Songs . Reports (song usage) Song usage for CCLI… under the song list. See CCLI report .","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#what-s-different","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"What’s different","text":"What’s different PraiseAV is free. No subscription, and as many computers as you like. Extra screens are built in. A preacher’s screen, a timer and a livestream feed each go on their own screen, without a subscription or splitter. PowerPoint isn’t needed to show PowerPoint files, and PDFs work too. Plan at home, run at church: keep services in OneDrive or Google Drive and they appear on the church computer. A phone remote works in any phone’s web browser, with nothing to install. See Phone remote .","summary":""},{"url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","heading":"","text":"Keyboard shortcuts You can run a whole service from the keyboard, or with a presentation clicker.","summary":"Every key PraiseAV uses: next and back, black, clear, logo, live video, notes pages, saving and help."},{"url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#running-a-service","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","heading":"Running a service","text":"Running a service Key What it does → ↓ Space Page Down Next slide (at the end of an item, the first slide of the next one) ← ↑ Page Up Previous slide B or . Black screen (press again to come back) C Clear text : hide the words, keep the background L Logo : show your church logo V Live video on or off [ and ] The preacher’s notes: previous and next page Tip: Presentation clickers send Page Down , Page Up and usually B or . for their blank-screen button, so they work with PraiseAV without any setting up.","summary":""},{"url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#everywhere-else","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","heading":"Everywhere else","text":"Everywhere else Key What it does F1 Help about what you’re doing Ctrl + S Save the service Esc Close a window or dialog Enter (in the Bible tab) Add the reading to the service Shift + Enter (in the Bible tab) Add the reading and show it now","summary":""},{"url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#in-the-slide-designer","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","heading":"In the slide designer","text":"In the slide designer Key What it does Arrow keys Nudge the selected item (hold Shift to move one pixel) Delete Remove the selected item Ctrl + D Copy the selected item Ctrl + Z Undo Esc Deselect (press again to close) The keys don’t do anything while you’re typing in a box, so you can’t change the screen by accident while searching for a song.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html","page":"Songs","heading":"","text":"Songs Your songs live in the Songs tab on the left. PraiseAV comes with some well-known hymns, and you can bring in your own from SongSelect, EasyWorship, OpenSong or a text file.","summary":"Find, add, edit, import and export songs: SongSelect, EasyWorship, OpenSong, OpenLyrics and plain lyrics files. Labels, slides, song order, backing tracks and videos."},{"url":"songs.html#find-a-song","page":"Songs","heading":"Find a song","text":"Find a song Type into the search box: a few words of the title , the words , or the author . You don’t need the start of the title: “saved a wretch” finds Amazing Grace .","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#add-a-song-to-the-service","page":"Songs","heading":"Add a song to the service","text":"Add a song to the service Any of these: Double-click the song. Point at the song and click + . Drag it into the service, to the place you want. New items go just after whatever is selected in the service.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#make-a-new-song-or-change-one","page":"Songs","heading":"Make a new song, or change one","text":"Make a new song, or change one Click + New (top of the Songs tab), or select a song in the service and click Edit song above its slides. You can also point at a song in the list and click ✎ . Box What to put Title The song’s name Author Who wrote it (shown small on the first slide) CCLI song number From SongSelect or the songbook. Needed for your CCLI report . Copyright For example “Public Domain” or “© 2019 Publisher” Song order Optional. The order to sing the sections, for example V1 C V2 C B C . Leave it blank to go in the order written. Backing track or video Optional. Music, or a video that plays behind the words, when the song goes on screen. See Music and audio . Lyrics The words (see below)","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#writing-the-words","page":"Songs","heading":"Writing the words","text":"Writing the words Put each section’s name in square brackets on its own line: [Verse 1] , [Chorus] , [Bridge] , [Pre-Chorus] , [Tag] . A blank line starts a new slide within a section. Long sections are split into slides of about four lines by themselves, evenly. [Verse 1] Amazing grace! How sweet the sound That saved a wretch like me! [Chorus] ... The preview on the right shows the slides as you type. Click Save song . Tip: With a song order like V1 C V2 C , you only type the chorus once, and it’s shown each time it comes round.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#chords-for-the-band","page":"Songs","heading":"Chords for the band","text":"Chords for the band Click Chords for the band in the song window to add chords. Put each chord in square brackets just before the word (or syllable) it goes over: [G]Amazing [G7]grace! How [C]sweet the [G]sound That saved a wretch like [D]me! The chords then appear above the words on the band’s screen . Songs imported from SongSelect chord charts and OpenSong bring their chords with them, and Amazing Grace comes with its traditional chords.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#delete-a-song","page":"Songs","heading":"Delete a song","text":"Delete a song Open it in the editor and click Delete song . It’s taken out of the library (and today’s service). Saved services keep their own copy, so they still work.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#import-songs","page":"Songs","heading":"Import songs","text":"Import songs Click Import in the Songs tab and choose the files (or a zip), or drag files or a whole folder onto the song list. PraiseAV understands: From What to use SongSelect (CCLI) Download the chord chart (PDF) or the lyrics (TXT) file from SongSelect, then import it. The CCLI number, author, copyright and key come too. EasyWorship From EasyWorship finds its whole song library on this computer. Or import a Songs.db file, or a schedule ( .ewsx ). See Coming from EasyWorship . OpenSong From OpenSong (shown when OpenSong is on this computer), or import its song files or drag in its Songs folder. OpenLyrics ( .xml ) The free song format that OpenLP, FreeShow, Quelea and PraiseAV’s own export use. A zip, or a folder A zip of song files (a download of chord charts, say), or a whole folder dragged onto the song list. PraiseAV looks through it, including folders inside, and imports every song file it finds. No need to unzip first. A text file A .txt file of lyrics, with section names like Verse 1 or [Chorus] on their own lines. When it’s done, PraiseAV lists what it brought in, what it skipped (songs you already have) and anything that didn’t work, with the reason. Note: Showing song words in church needs a CCLI Church Copyright Licence (or permission for each song), however you got the words. Traditional hymns like Amazing Grace are free to use.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#export-songs","page":"Songs","heading":"Export songs","text":"Export songs Your songs are never locked in. Click Export songs… at the bottom of the Songs tab and choose where to put them. PraiseAV makes a folder called PraiseAV songs with the date, and inside it every song twice: Text : a .txt file for each song, easy to read and print, laid out like a SongSelect lyrics file. OpenLyrics : an .xml file for each song, in the free standard format that other worship programs (OpenLP, FreeShow, Quelea…) can import, with verses, song order, authors, copyright and CCLI number. Either kind can be imported back into PraiseAV on another computer. To move everything (services, settings and media too), use a backup instead.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#themes-for-a-song","page":"Songs","heading":"Themes for a song","text":"Themes for a song How the words look comes from the theme for songs. To give one song a different look (a Christmas carol, say), pick a theme in the box above its slides. See Themes .","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html","page":"The Bible","heading":"","text":"The Bible The Bible tab finds readings as you type, and puts them on screen with the reference and translation on every slide.","summary":"Find readings by reference or words, choose translations, show the NIV by copying from bible.com, and choose how much goes on each slide."},{"url":"bible.html#find-a-reading","page":"The Bible","heading":"Find a reading","text":"Find a reading Type the reference into the box. Short forms are fine: You type You get jn 3 16 John 3:16 john 3:16-18 John 3:16–18 ps 23 Psalm 23 (the whole psalm) 1 cor 13 4-7 1 Corinthians 13:4–7 rom 8:28-9:1 Romans 8:28 to 9:1 Or type a few words (“good shepherd”) to find verses that contain them. Click a result to open it. Then: Enter or Add to service adds the reading to the service. Shift + Enter or Show now adds it and puts it on screen straight away.","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html#translations","page":"The Bible","heading":"Translations","text":"Translations PraiseAV includes three translations, which work without the internet: BSB : the Berean Standard Bible, modern English (the default) KJV : the King James Version WEB : the World English Bible Choose the translation in the box next to the search. A reading already in the service can be switched to another translation with the box above its slides.","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html#the-niv-and-other-translations","page":"The Bible","heading":"The NIV and other translations","text":"The NIV and other translations The NIV, ESV, NLT and others can’t be included in PraiseAV, but you can show the verses your service uses: Type the reference, then click NIV and more from bible.com . bible.com opens in its own window, at that chapter. Tap the verses you want (they’re underlined), then press bible.com’s Copy . The verses appear in PraiseAV’s Bible tab. Click Add to service or Show now . You can also copy verses from Bible Gateway or anywhere else, and click Paste verses (or paste into the search box). Include the reference if it’s not from bible.com. Copied verses are kept with the service, so they work at church without the internet. Every slide shows the translation after the reference, like “(NIV)”. That’s what the NIV’s publisher asks for on church slides. Note: Churches can quote up to 500 verses of the NIV without asking permission, with the translation named. A Sunday’s readings are well within that.","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html#bible-settings","page":"The Bible","heading":"Bible settings","text":"Bible settings Open Bible settings at the bottom of the Bible tab: How much of a reading goes on each slide: As much as fits (the default), Shorter slides , or One verse per slide . Translations to show in the lists: untick the ones your church doesn’t use. (The translation in use can’t be hidden.)","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html#how-readings-look","page":"The Bible","heading":"How readings look","text":"How readings look Readings use the theme for Bible verses. Many churches choose High contrast (easy to read) for readings. See Themes .","summary":""},{"url":"services.html","page":"Services","heading":"","text":"Services A service is the order of everything for one meeting: songs, readings, the sermon, videos, notices. It’s the second column of the window.","summary":"Build a service, save it, open it on another computer through OneDrive, Google Drive or a USB stick, and start a new one."},{"url":"services.html#building-a-service","page":"Services","heading":"Building a service","text":"Building a service Songs: double-click in the Songs tab, or drag them in. See Songs . Bible readings: from the Bible tab. See The Bible . The sermon: click + Sermon . See Sermons . Your own slides: click + Slides (notices, a welcome, a verse with a picture). See Slide designer . Videos, pictures, music, PowerPoint: from the Media tab. See Media . New items go just after the selected one. Drag items to change the order. To take one out, point at it and click ✕ . Each item shows what it is: Bible , Sermon , Video , Music or Slides (songs have no label).","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#start-and-end-times","page":"Services","heading":"Start and end times","text":"Start and end times Under the name, Starts and Ends set when the service begins and finishes. The congregation screen counts down to the start, and the preacher’s screen shows how long is left. See Timers and prompts .","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#saving","page":"Services","heading":"Saving","text":"Saving Type a name at the top of the Service column. PraiseAV suggests the coming Sunday’s date. Click Save (or press Ctrl + S ). The words under the buttons say whether it’s saved. Save turns blue when there are changes not yet saved. Tip: PraiseAV also keeps your current service as you work, all the time. If the power goes off, it’s still there when PraiseAV opens again.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#opening-a-saved-service","page":"Services","heading":"Opening a saved service","text":"Opening a saved service Click Open . Your saved services are listed, newest first. Click one to open it. A saved service carries its own copy of every song, so it works on another computer even if that computer doesn’t have the songs: they’re added to its library. Pictures and videos aren’t inside the file (they can be big). If one is missing, PraiseAV tells you which.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#plan-at-home-run-at-church","page":"Services","heading":"Plan at home, run at church","text":"Plan at home, run at church Keep services in a folder that both computers can see. In Open , under Where services are kept : Use OneDrive or Use Google Drive : services saved at home appear on the church computer by themselves, if both are signed in to the same account. (The buttons show when OneDrive or Google Drive is on this computer.) Choose a folder… : anywhere, for example a shared folder. Just this computer : back to normal.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#with-a-usb-stick","page":"Services","heading":"With a USB stick","text":"With a USB stick Save a copy to a USB stick… saves this service wherever you choose. Open from somewhere else (USB stick)… opens a service file from anywhere.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#starting-a-new-service","page":"Services","heading":"Starting a new service","text":"Starting a new service Click New . If this one has changes that aren’t saved, PraiseAV asks first. Songs stay in your library.","summary":""},{"url":"printing.html","page":"Printing","heading":"","text":"Printing Click Print in the Service column. There are two kinds of printout.","summary":"Print a service sheet for the congregation (A4 or a folded A5 booklet), or the running order for the team, or save either as a PDF."},{"url":"printing.html#a-service-sheet-for-the-congregation","page":"Printing","heading":"A service sheet, for the congregation","text":"A service sheet, for the congregation A handout with: your church’s logo and name , and the service’s name the order of service the words of each song , laid out by verse and chorus, with the author and CCLI number the Bible readings in full the sermon title, the preacher, and the main points with lines for notes (the points come from the sermon’s slides) notices , if you type some in (they’re kept with the service) the line “Song words reproduced under CCLI Licence No. …”, if you enter your church’s licence number Choose the size: A4 pages : printed as normal. A5 booklet (A4 folded in half) : PraiseAV arranges the pages so that, printed on both sides, the pile folds into a booklet with the pages in order. In the print window, choose print on both sides and flip on short edge . Tick Add a “My notes” page for a ruled page at the end.","summary":""},{"url":"printing.html#a-logo-for-paper","page":"Printing","heading":"A logo for paper","text":"A logo for paper A logo made for the screen often has white or light words, which disappear on white paper. If your church has a version with dark words, use that for printing: in the Media tab, click it and choose Use for printing . See Media . Note: Printing song words for the congregation is covered by a CCLI Church Copyright Licence . Enter your licence number so it’s printed on the sheet. PraiseAV counts printed songs for your CCLI report .","summary":""},{"url":"printing.html#the-running-order-for-the-team","page":"Printing","heading":"The running order, for the team","text":"The running order, for the team A list of everything in the service, with song details. Tick: Include song words : handy for the band Include the preacher’s notes","summary":""},{"url":"printing.html#saving-as-a-pdf","page":"Printing","heading":"Saving as a PDF","text":"Saving as a PDF Windows’ print window opens after you click Print… . To make a PDF (to email, or put on your website), choose the printer Microsoft Print to PDF .","summary":""},{"url":"ccli-report.html","page":"CCLI report","heading":"","text":"CCLI report If your church has a CCLI Church Copyright Licence , CCLI asks you to report the songs you use. PraiseAV keeps count for you. Click Song usage for CCLI… at the bottom of the Songs tab.","summary":"The songs you've used, shown on screen, printed and streamed, ready for your CCLI report, as a spreadsheet or printout."},{"url":"ccli-report.html#what-s-counted","page":"CCLI report","heading":"What’s counted","text":"What’s counted Shown: each song once per service, the first time it goes on screen. Printed: when you print song words (a service sheet, or a running order with words). Streamed: when a song is shown while the Livestream screen is open or an ATEM is connected. This is for a CCLI Streaming Licence .","summary":""},{"url":"ccli-report.html#choosing-the-dates","page":"CCLI report","heading":"Choosing the dates","text":"Choosing the dates Click Last 6 months , Last 12 months , This year , Last year , or Choose dates .","summary":""},{"url":"ccli-report.html#using-it","page":"CCLI report","heading":"Using it","text":"Using it Save as spreadsheet… saves the list as a file that opens in Excel, LibreOffice or Google Sheets. Print… prints it. Copy the songs into CCLI’s online reporting, or keep the file for your records. Tip: Songs without a CCLI number are marked “none”. Old hymns are often public domain and don’t need reporting. For a song that is covered, add its number in the song editor and it appears in the report. The counts are kept on your computer only. PraiseAV doesn’t send anything to CCLI.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html","page":"Running a service","heading":"","text":"Running a service On Sunday you mostly need two things: click a slide to put it on screen, and Next to move on.","summary":"Putting slides on screen, Next and Previous, Up next, Black, Clear text and Logo, timed words, videos, the corner logo and clickers."},{"url":"running-a-service.html#putting-something-on-screen","page":"Running a service","heading":"Putting something on screen","text":"Putting something on screen Click an item in the Service column. Its slides appear in the middle. Click a slide. It’s on the screen now: it gets a red border, and the item gets a LIVE label.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#next-and-previous","page":"Running a service","heading":"Next and Previous","text":"Next and Previous Next (or → , Space , Page Down , or a clicker) shows the next slide. At the end of an item, Next goes on to the first slide of the next item in the service. Previous (or ← ) goes back.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#on-screen-and-up-next","page":"Running a service","heading":"On screen and Up next","text":"On screen and Up next The right-hand column shows: On screen : exactly what the congregation sees now, with a red border. Now and Next : in words. Up next : a small picture of exactly what Next will put on the screen. Tip: Glance at Up next before pressing Next, especially at the end of a song. You’ll never be caught out.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#black-clear-text-and-logo","page":"Running a service","heading":"Black, Clear text and Logo","text":"Black, Clear text and Logo Button Key What happens Black B The screen goes black. Use it between items, or if anything goes wrong. Clear text C The words go, the background stays. Good for a moment of reflection. Logo L Your church logo. Good before and after the service. Press the button again (or the key) to come back. Clicking any slide comes back too. Choose your logo in the Media tab: click a picture and Use as logo . See Media .","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#timed-words-a-song-that-plays-itself","page":"Running a service","heading":"Timed words (a song that plays itself)","text":"Timed words (a song that plays itself) When a song plays its own recorded music, a backing track or video , the words can move on by themselves in time with it, like a karaoke video. Record the timing once, by pressing Next along with the music: Select the song in the service. Above its slides, click ⏱ Record timing . The song goes on screen from the top, and its video or backing track starts from the beginning. Press Next (or the clicker, a pedal, or the phone remote) in time with the music, just as you would live. At the end, click Done . PraiseAV says how many slide changes it recorded. From then on, Words move by themselves is ticked. When the song comes on screen (from its first slide), the words move on as recorded, and a TIMED label shows. ▶ From the top starts the song and its music again with the timing. If you press Next , Previous or click a slide yourself while it’s timed, the timing stops and you’re in charge again. Black , Clear text and Logo don’t stop it. Not right? Click Record again . Untick Words move by themselves to move the words by hand in this service. The timing is kept with the song , so it’s ready the next time you add the song to a service. It only runs when the song’s backing track or video is playing: if you untick Play the backing track/video (the band is playing it live), the words wait for Next as usual. Note: Timed words are for recorded music, which is the same every time. A live band, which may repeat a chorus or carry on as the Spirit leads, should always have the words moved by a person (or by the worship leader with a pedal or phone ).","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#videos","page":"Running a service","heading":"Videos","text":"Videos When a video is on screen, buttons appear under On screen : Pause / Play and From the start . It says whether the video loops or plays once. To change that, tick Loop above the video’s slides. Videos play with sound on the projector’s computer (to your sound desk). The video in PraiseAV’s own window is silent.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#the-logo-in-the-corner","page":"Running a service","heading":"The logo in the corner","text":"The logo in the corner Some churches like their logo small in a corner all the time, like a TV channel. Open Live video on the right, and tick Church logo in the corner , then choose which corner. The words move to stay clear of it. If your logo has words in it, they’ll be small up there. You can choose a different picture for the corner, such as just your symbol: see Media .","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#using-a-clicker","page":"Running a service","heading":"Using a clicker","text":"Using a clicker Presentation clickers (like the ones for PowerPoint) work straight away. Forward is Next , back is Previous , and the “blank screen” button is Black . Most work from the back of the room, so the person running PraiseAV can sit with everyone. The phone remote does the same from a phone.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#hiding-all-the-screens","page":"Running a service","heading":"Hiding all the screens","text":"Hiding all the screens Hide (top right) takes all PraiseAV’s screens away, for example to use the projector for something else. Show brings them back.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"","text":"Running a service without an AV volunteer Small churches don’t always have someone for the laptop, and sometimes a volunteer can’t come. PraiseAV can be run from the front with phones: the worship leader runs the songs and readings from their phone (the AV remote ) the preacher puts their sermon on screen and moves their own slides and notes (the preacher remote ) The laptop just sits to one side, plugged in.","summary":"No one free to sit at the laptop? The worship leader runs the words and the preacher runs their sermon, each from their own phone."},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#set-it-up-once-before-the-day","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"Set it up once, before the day","text":"Set it up once, before the day Do this when someone is at the laptop, for example at a rehearsal. It only needs doing once for each phone. On the laptop, click Remote (top right), then Switch remote control on . The worship leader scans the code with their phone, types their name and taps Connect . On the laptop, click Allow as AV remote . The preacher does the same. On the laptop, click Allow as preacher remote . PraiseAV remembers both phones. From then on they connect straight away, with nobody needed at the laptop. Remote control also switches itself back on each time PraiseAV opens, if it was on last time. See Phone and tablet remote for more, and what to do if a phone can’t connect.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#on-the-day","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"On the day","text":"On the day Plug the laptop in (see the battery below), open PraiseAV, and open the service. Put the welcome slide or logo on screen, then leave the laptop. The worship leader opens the remote on their phone. They tap a song in the service, then Next ▶ for each verse. Black and Logo are there for in between. When it’s time for the sermon , the preacher taps ▶ Start my sermon on their phone. The sermon goes on screen, with their notes on their phone and the preacher’s screen. The preacher uses Slide ▶ and Notes ▶ as they go. (The preacher remote can’t move away from the sermon, so they can’t knock the songs out by accident.) After the sermon, the worship leader taps the next song. Tip: Agree beforehand who taps what, especially who starts the sermon and who takes over afterwards.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#the-laptop-stays-awake","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"The laptop stays awake","text":"The laptop stays awake While PraiseAV is showing the screens, or remote control is on, the laptop won’t go to sleep or turn the projector off, however long nobody touches it.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#the-battery","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"The battery","text":"The battery When the laptop is running on its battery, a small note at the top of PraiseAV says so ( On battery · 80% ). As it runs down it turns amber, and then red with plug in the laptop . Phones connected to the remote see Laptop battery 25%: plug it in at the top when it gets low, so the people at the front know too. Important: Plug the laptop in before the service. A projector and phones connected for an hour can use a lot of battery.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#one-person-leading-worship-and-preaching","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"One person leading worship and preaching","text":"One person leading worship and preaching If the worship leader also preaches, allow their phone as an AV remote . At the bottom of the remote, ⇄ Switch to the preacher’s view gives them their notes, timer and Start my sermon , and ⇄ Switch to the AV view takes them back to the songs. There’s no pairing again. See Phone remote . The same button is handy for demos: one phone shows both remotes.","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"","text":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen A sermon has two parts, moved on separately: Slides for the congregation on the front screen: the title, points, pictures. The preacher’s notes , only on the preacher’s screen (usually a TV at the back, where the preacher can see it).","summary":"Add a sermon with slides for the congregation and private notes for the preacher, and show them on the preacher's screen at the back."},{"url":"sermons.html#adding-a-sermon","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"Adding a sermon","text":"Adding a sermon Click + Sermon at the top of the Service column. Title and Speaker . What the congregation sees first : the church logo, the sermon title, or a sermon picture. Slides : add words (points, verses) and pictures (from the Media tab). They’re shown in order with Next . Notes : the preacher’s notes, only on the back screen. A line with just --- starts a new page. Click Save . To change it later, select the sermon and click Edit sermon above its slides. Tip: If the preacher sends a PowerPoint, add it from the Media tab instead (see PowerPoint and PDF ), and put their notes in a sermon.","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html#during-the-sermon","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"During the sermon","text":"During the sermon Next moves the congregation’s slides . Notes ▶ and ◀ Notes (or the ] and [ keys) move the preacher’s notes , separately. Tick Notes follow slides if the preacher wants each slide to bring its own notes page (slide 1 with page 1, and so on). Hide notes puts the back screen back to showing the slides.","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html#the-preacher-s-screen","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"The preacher’s screen","text":"The preacher’s screen It shows, from top to bottom: the clock and the timer (see Timers and prompts ) any prompt from the desk, like “Wrap up”, big and bright the notes page, with “Page 1 of 4” what’s on screen now for the congregation, and what’s next","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html#words-for-the-band","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"Words for the band","text":"Words for the band The same screen is the band’s words during songs. When a song is on screen, the words fill the preacher’s screen, big and bold, with the next line along the bottom, so the worship leader and band can sing without turning round. The sermon notes come back when you go back to the sermon. If a song has chords, they show above the words (in yellow), each over the right syllable:","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html#choosing-what-it-shows","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"Choosing what it shows","text":"Choosing what it shows In Screens… , under Preacher screen , tick what you want: During songs, big words for the band, with the next line underneath Chords above the words (for songs that have them; see chords ) Clock Timer (when one is running) Prompts from the desk What’s on the screen and what’s next, along the bottom And the Text size : Normal , Large or Extra large (good for a screen far away, or for older eyes). The preacher’s screen changes as you tick, so you can see the difference. These choices go in the church setup with everything else. To set up the preacher’s screen, see Screens . There’s also a preacher’s phone remote , so the preacher can move their own slides and notes.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html","page":"Timers and prompts","heading":"","text":"Timers and prompts Open Timer and prompts in the right-hand column.","summary":"Sermon countdowns, counting down to the start of the service, stopwatches, and quiet prompts like \"5 minutes left\" for the preacher."},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#service-start-and-end-times","page":"Timers and prompts","heading":"Service start and end times","text":"Service start and end times Under the service’s name are Starts and Ends . Type the times once (10:30 and 11:45, say); new services get the same times, so most weeks there’s nothing to change. Before the service , the congregation screen counts down: Service starts in 4:59 , small in a corner over the notices or the logo. It goes as soon as the first item is put on screen. During the service , the preacher’s and timer screens show Ends 11:45 · 23 min left , amber near the end and red if it runs over. A prompt, 10 minutes to the end of the service , appears on the platform screens at that point. The printed service sheet shows the start time. In Timer and prompts , under Service times , choose the countdown ( in a corner , full screen , or none ) and when the end prompt comes (or untick it). Nothing stops by itself at the end: if the service runs on as the Spirit leads, the screens just say how far over it is.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#timers","page":"Timers and prompts","heading":"Timers","text":"Timers Start sermon timer : type the minutes (say 25 ) and click. It counts down. Count down to time : type a time like 10:30 for “Service starts in…”, before the service. Stopwatch : counts up. While a timer runs: ❚❚ Pause / ▶ Resume , +1 min and −1 min , ↺ Restart and Stop . The timer shows on the preacher’s screen and the timer screen . It turns amber in the last few minutes and red when time is up (it keeps counting, so you can see how far over). Tick Also show the countdown on the congregation screen to show it to everyone, for example “Service starts in 4:59” before the service.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#prompts","page":"Timers and prompts","heading":"Prompts","text":"Prompts Prompts are quiet messages for the preacher or the band. They show on the preacher’s screen and timer screen only, never to the congregation. Quick prompts: 5 minutes left , 2 minutes left , Wrap up , Slow down , Mic is off . Or type your own and click Show . Clear takes it away.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#the-automatic-prompt","page":"Timers and prompts","heading":"The automatic prompt","text":"The automatic prompt PraiseAV can show “5 minutes left” by itself when the sermon timer gets there. Tick Automatically show and set how many minutes, or untick it to switch it off.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#a-timer-screen","page":"Timers and prompts","heading":"A timer screen","text":"A timer screen Some churches put a big timer where the preacher or band can see it (a TV on the floor at the front, say). In Screens… , give that screen the Timer job. See Screens .","summary":""},{"url":"parent-alerts.html","page":"Parent alerts and notices","heading":"","text":"Parent alerts and notices Open Parent alerts in the right-hand column.","summary":"Call a parent from the crèche or children's groups with a number on screen, and scroll short notices like \"car lights left on\"."},{"url":"parent-alerts.html#calling-a-parent","page":"Parent alerts and notices","heading":"Calling a parent","text":"Calling a parent Click the group: for example Crèche or Sunday School . Type the child’s number (or name). Click Show on screen (or press Enter ). A banner appears on the congregation screen, over whatever is showing, and the words make room for it: It stays for the number of minutes set (5 to start with), then goes by itself. To take it off sooner, click ✕ next to it in the list. Clear all alerts takes them all away.","summary":""},{"url":"parent-alerts.html#scrolling-notices","page":"Parent alerts and notices","heading":"Scrolling notices","text":"Scrolling notices Type a short notice (“Would the owner of the blue car please see a steward”) and click Scroll . It scrolls across the bottom of the screen a few times, then stops.","summary":""},{"url":"parent-alerts.html#your-own-group-names","page":"Parent alerts and notices","heading":"Your own group names","text":"Your own group names Click Settings in Parent alerts. Rename the groups to what your church calls them: Explorers, Little Lambs, Youth… Add or remove groups. The wording : for example {group}: could the parent of {number} please come . {group} and {number} are filled in. Where on the screen (along the top or bottom) and the Colour . How long a parent alert stays (1 to 10 minutes, or until you clear it), and how many times notices go across. Click Save . Back to the standard wording undoes changes to the wording. Note: Alerts only show on the congregation screen, never on the Livestream screen, so children’s numbers don’t go out on your stream. (If your stream films the main screen itself, viewers will see them.) Alerts aren’t saved anywhere once they’re cleared.","summary":""},{"url":"controllers.html","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"","text":"MIDI and Stream Deck Besides the keyboard, a clicker and the phone remote , PraiseAV can be run from: a MIDI foot pedal, keyboard or pad controller: handy for a worship leader who plays and moves the words themselves a Stream Deck (Elgato’s box of buttons), or Bitfocus Companion Click MIDI and Stream Deck… at the bottom right of PraiseAV.","summary":"Run PraiseAV from a MIDI foot pedal, keyboard or pad controller, or from Stream Deck buttons (or Bitfocus Companion)."},{"url":"controllers.html#midi","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"MIDI","text":"MIDI Plug the MIDI device into the computer (USB). A keyboard with a USB socket, a foot pedal with a USB MIDI box, or a small pad controller all work. Tick Use MIDI . PraiseAV lists the devices it found. For each action you want, click Learn , then press the key, pedal or pad for it. It shows what it learnt, for example Note C4 (60) or Controller 64 . The actions: Next , Previous , Black , Clear text , Logo , Notes ▶ , ◀ Notes and Start the sermon . Click ✕ to forget one. PraiseAV remembers the rest on this computer. Tip: A sustain pedal plugged into a keyboard sends Controller 64 : learn it for Next and the keyboard player can move the words with their foot.","summary":""},{"url":"controllers.html#stream-deck","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"Stream Deck","text":"Stream Deck","summary":""},{"url":"controllers.html#the-simple-way-hotkeys","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"The simple way: hotkeys","text":"The simple way: hotkeys In the Stream Deck app, make Hotkey buttons with PraiseAV’s keys: Button Hotkey Next → (right arrow) Previous ← (left arrow) Black B Clear text C Logo L Notes ▶ / ◀ Notes ] and [ Live video on or off V PraiseAV must be the window in front for hotkeys to work.","summary":""},{"url":"controllers.html#buttons-that-always-work-web-addresses","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"Buttons that always work: web addresses","text":"Buttons that always work: web addresses Each action also has a web address, which works even when PraiseAV isn’t in front, or from another computer running Bitfocus Companion. In MIDI and Stream Deck… , click Switch remote control on if it isn’t on already. Click Copy next to an action to copy its address. In the Stream Deck app, use a free web request plugin from the Stream Deck store (search “web request”), and paste the address into a button. In Bitfocus Companion , use the Generic HTTP module with a GET request. The addresses contain a secret key, so only your buttons can use them. Make a new key if you think someone else has the addresses: the old ones stop working. The copied addresses start http://127.0.0.1 , for a Stream Deck plugged into the PraiseAV computer. For Companion on another computer, use the address shown in the window instead.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"","text":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds The Media tab holds your videos, pictures and music: moving backgrounds, your logo, notices, clips.","summary":"Add videos, pictures and music, choose the background behind song words, choose your church logo, folders, slideshows and see-through videos."},{"url":"media.html#free-starter-backgrounds","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Free starter backgrounds","text":"Free starter backgrounds No backgrounds yet? Click + Free starter backgrounds (in All or Backgrounds ). PraiseAV makes twelve on this computer, in about half a minute, into Backgrounds › Starter : six calm moving loops: Soft Light , Gentle Waves , Aurora , Night Sky , Warm Glow and Morning Mist six still ones: Deep Blue , Royal Purple , Sunrise , Forest , Slate and Warm Sand PraiseAV draws them itself, so they’re free to use for anything, with nothing to license. The moving ones loop smoothly, with no jump.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#where-the-files-live","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Where the files live","text":"Where the files live PraiseAV keeps media in one folder: Documents › PraiseAV Media to start with. The path is shown under the buttons. Inside are folders for each kind: Backgrounds : what goes behind song words and verses. Logos : your church logo. Videos and Pictures : clips, notices, photos. Music and Audio : walk-in music, backing tracks. The chips along the top ( All , Backgrounds , Logos …) show one folder at a time. Open folder opens it in File Explorer. Change folder… uses a different folder, for example where your old EasyWorship backgrounds are, or a shared folder.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#adding-files","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Adding files","text":"Adding files Click + Add videos, pictures or music , or drag files from File Explorer onto the list. They’re copied into the media folder, so they still work after a USB stick is unplugged. Music goes into Music and Audio by itself. Kind Files that work Videos MP4, M4V, MOV, WEBM, OGV Pictures JPG, PNG, GIF, WEBP, BMP, SVG Music MP3, M4A, AAC, WAV, OGG, OPUS, FLAC Careful: Some old video files (WMV, AVI, or ProRes from a Mac) won’t play. PraiseAV says so if one doesn’t. The free HandBrake app converts them to MP4, which always works.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#using-a-file","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Using a file","text":"Using a file Click a file to choose it. Buttons appear underneath: Add to service (or double-click the file, or drag it into the service). Use as background : shows it behind song words and Bible verses. It gets a Background label. Use as logo : shown when you press Logo ( L ). It gets a Logo label. Use in the corner : a different picture for the small logo in the corner of the screen. It gets a Corner logo label. Use for printing (pictures): a different picture for printed service sheets. It gets a Print logo label. Play over the screen (videos): see below. ▶ Play now (music): see Music and audio .","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#your-church-background","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Your church background","text":"Your church background A gentle moving background behind the words looks good and is easy to read. Choose one in Backgrounds , then Use as background . Themes can use a different one (see Themes ).","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#your-church-logo","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Your church logo","text":"Your church logo Put your logo in Logos , click it, then Use as logo . A PNG with a see-through background looks best. It also appears on printed service sheets and can sit small in a corner of the screen (see Running a service ).","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#a-different-logo-for-the-corner","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"A different logo for the corner","text":"A different logo for the corner A logo with words looks good full screen, but in the small corner the words become hard to read. Many churches use just their symbol there (the cross, the heart, the dove…). Make a picture of just the symbol, and add it to Logos . Click it, then Use in the corner . The full logo is still used for Logo ( L ) and the service sheet. Stop using in the corner puts the full logo back in the corner.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#a-logo-for-printing","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"A logo for printing","text":"A logo for printing Logos for screens usually have light words, made to stand out on a dark background. On white paper those words disappear. Many churches have a second version with dark words for paper. Add the dark version to Logos . Click it, then Use for printing . Printed service sheets then use it, and the screens keep the light one. Stop using for printing puts the church logo back on the sheets.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#sing-along-with-a-worship-video","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Sing along with a worship video","text":"Sing along with a worship video A worship video of a band playing, with no words on it? Put the song’s words over it so everyone can sing along: Click the video, then Sing along with a song… . Type the song’s title (or some words), and click it in the list. The song must be in the Songs tab already. The song goes into the service with the video as its backing video . When it’s on screen, the video plays behind the words with its sound. Move the words with Next , or record the timing once so they move by themselves. To show the video on its own, without words, add the video itself to the service instead.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#folders-inside-folders","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Folders inside folders","text":"Folders inside folders In Backgrounds (or any folder), click + Folder to make one inside it, for example Christmas or Easter . Then click it and add files. All in Backgrounds shows them all again.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#a-slideshow-of-pictures","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"A slideshow of pictures","text":"A slideshow of pictures Open a folder with several pictures and click Make a slideshow of these pictures . They go into the service as one item that moves on by itself every 8 seconds. See Announcement loops .","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#see-through-videos-over-the-screen","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"See-through videos over the screen","text":"See-through videos over the screen Some videos are made to go on top : confetti, snow, falling leaves, light leaks. Choose one and click Play over the screen . It plays over whatever is showing, and the words stay. Under On screen , tick Loop or click Stop . These need a video with a see-through background (usually WEBM). Ordinary videos would cover everything.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#videos-in-the-service","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Videos in the service","text":"Videos in the service A video in the service shows as one slide. Click it to play. See Running a service .","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#good-background-clips","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Good background clips","text":"Good background clips If you’re choosing or buying moving backgrounds: 1920 × 1080 (Full HD), MP4 (H.264). 4K looks no better on most projectors and makes the computer work harder. Seamless loops of 15 to 60 seconds, so there’s no jump when they go round. Slow and calm , dark or soft colours, nothing busy in the middle where the words go. Keep them under about 50 MB each. Free sources include Pixabay and Pexels (search “abstract loop” or “worship background”). Check each site’s licence.","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html","page":"Music and audio","heading":"","text":"Music and audio PraiseAV can play music through the computer’s sound output, usually a cable to the sound desk.","summary":"Walk-in music, music cues in the service, backing tracks that start with a song, fade out, volume and loop."},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#walk-in-music","page":"Music and audio","heading":"Walk-in music","text":"Walk-in music Put your music in the Media tab, in Music and Audio (click + Add videos, pictures or music ). Click Music and Audio , then ▶ Play all . The tracks play one after another. Or click one track and ▶ Play now .","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#controlling-it","page":"Music and audio","heading":"Controlling it","text":"Controlling it Open Music and audio in the right-hand column: ▶ Play / ❚❚ Pause . Fade out : turns it down gently over 3 seconds, then stops. Use it when the service starts. ■ Stop : stops at once. Skip ▶▶ : next track. Drag the bar to jump to a point in the track. Volume , and Loop to go round again when it ends. The top line always says what’s playing and how long is left.","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#music-in-the-service","page":"Music and audio","heading":"Music in the service","text":"Music in the service Drag a music file from the Media tab into the service, for example a track for the offering. It shows with a Music label. Click it and press the big ▶ Play button. The screens don’t change, so you can play music under a slide.","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#backing-tracks-and-videos","page":"Music and audio","heading":"Backing tracks and videos","text":"Backing tracks and videos If the band is away, a song can have a backing track (an MP3, say) or a video (a worship video of a band playing) that plays when the song goes on screen. Add the music or video in the Media tab. In the Songs tab, choose the song and click Edit song . Under Backing track or video , choose the file. Save. When the song goes on screen, the track or video starts from the beginning. A video plays behind the words, with its sound , so everyone can sing along, like a karaoke video. When you move on to the next item, it stops. For a video there’s a quicker way: in the Media tab, click the video, then Sing along with a song… and choose the song. See Media . A week when the band plays it live? Above the song’s slides, untick Play the backing track (or Play the backing video ). The song is then just the words, moved with Next as usual. It only changes that service. The words can also move on by themselves in time with the track or video: see Timed words . Note: Spotify, Apple Music and YouTube Music can’t be played through PraiseAV: their apps don’t allow it. Buy the tracks as MP3s or use your own recordings, and check your licence covers playing them in church (in the UK, CCLI can arrange the PRS for Music Church Licence).","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#the-sound","page":"Music and audio","heading":"The sound","text":"The sound Music plays through Windows’ default sound output. To choose which (say, the headphone socket to the sound desk, or the HDMI to the projector), click the speaker icon by the clock at the bottom right of Windows. Videos’ sound comes from the congregation screen’s window, through the same output.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"","text":"PowerPoint and PDF A visiting speaker brings slides on a USB stick? Add them to the service.","summary":"Bring in a guest speaker's PowerPoint or a PDF. Each slide becomes a picture in the service. Needs PowerPoint or the free LibreOffice for PowerPoint files."},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#adding-slides","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"Adding slides","text":"Adding slides In the Media tab, click + Add PowerPoint or PDF and choose the file. Or drag the file onto the service. PraiseAV turns each slide into a picture. It says how far it’s got (“Making slides… 4 of 12”), then adds the slides to the service. Click a slide to put it on screen, and use Next as normal. The slides are kept in the Media tab under Presentations , so you can add them to another service.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#which-files","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"Which files","text":"Which files PDF : always works. PowerPoint (PPTX, PPT, PPS, PPSX), LibreOffice (ODP) and Keynote (KEY): need Microsoft PowerPoint or the free LibreOffice on this computer. PraiseAV uses PowerPoint if it’s there (closest to how the slides were made), otherwise LibreOffice. If neither is installed, PraiseAV offers to open the LibreOffice download page. Install it once, then add the PowerPoint again. Tip: Ask speakers to send a PDF too (in PowerPoint: File › Export › PDF ). It’s quicker and always looks right.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#what-doesn-t-come-across","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"What doesn’t come across","text":"What doesn’t come across Each slide becomes a still picture, so: Animations (points appearing one by one) and transitions aren’t kept. Each slide shows as it looks at the end. Videos inside the PowerPoint don’t play. Ask for the video file separately and add it from the Media tab. The speaker’s notes aren’t brought in. Paste them into a sermon to show on the preacher’s screen.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#moving-on-by-itself","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"Moving on by itself","text":"Moving on by itself Tick Loop by itself above the slides to make them move on every few seconds and go round again: good for a notices presentation before the service. See Announcement loops .","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html","page":"Slide designer","heading":"","text":"Slide designer For a welcome slide, notices, or a verse over a picture, make your own slides.","summary":"Make your own slides: a welcome, notices, a verse with a picture. Words, pictures, boxes and backgrounds, with ready-made layouts."},{"url":"slide-designer.html#making-slides","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Making slides","text":"Making slides Click + Slides at the top of the Service column. The slide designer opens. On the left : the slides. Click one to change it. Point at a slide for ↑ and ↓ (move), ⧉ (copy) and ✕ (delete). In the middle : the slide itself. On the right : settings for whatever you’ve clicked. Type a name at the top (“Welcome”, “Notices”), then Save slides . They go into the service. To change them later, select the item and click Edit slides above its slides.","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#a-new-slide","page":"Slide designer","heading":"A new slide","text":"A new slide Click + New slide and pick a layout to start from: Title Title and text Picture with caption Words on a band Big verse Blank","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#adding-things","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Adding things","text":"Adding things At the top: + Words , + Picture , + Box . Drag to move. Drag a corner to resize. Lines appear when things line up with the middle or each other. Arrow keys nudge (hold Shift for one pixel at a time). Delete removes, Ctrl + D copies, Ctrl + Z undoes (or ↶ Undo ).","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#words","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Words","text":"Words Click the words, then type in the box on the right. Choose the Font , Size , colour, B (bold), I (italic), Shadow , and Left , Centre or Right .","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#pictures","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Pictures","text":"Pictures Choose which picture (from the Media tab), and Whole picture (shows all of it) or Fill the box (crops to fit). Rounded corners if you like.","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#boxes","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Boxes","text":"Boxes A coloured box, for example a band behind words. Choose the colour, See-through and Rounded corners .","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#arrange","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Arrange","text":"Arrange Centre across , Centre down , Bring forward , Send back , Copy , Delete .","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#the-slide-background","page":"Slide designer","heading":"The slide background","text":"The slide background Click an empty part of the slide. On the right, under Slide background : The usual background : your church background (from the Media tab). A colour . A picture or video from the Media tab.","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#moving-on-by-itself","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Moving on by itself","text":"Moving on by itself For notices before the service, tick Loop by itself above the slides. See Announcement loops .","summary":""},{"url":"announcement-loops.html","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"","text":"Announcement loops Before the service, it’s good to have the notices going round on the screen by themselves while people come in.","summary":"Notices that move on by themselves and go round again before the service, from your own slides, pictures or a PowerPoint."},{"url":"announcement-loops.html#making-one","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"Making one","text":"Making one Any of these can loop: Your own slides : click + Slides and make them. See Slide designer . Pictures : in the Media tab, open a folder of notice pictures and click Make a slideshow of these pictures . A PowerPoint or PDF of notices. See PowerPoint and PDF .","summary":""},{"url":"announcement-loops.html#turning-on-the-loop","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"Turning on the loop","text":"Turning on the loop Select the item in the service. Above its slides: Tick Loop by itself . Choose how long each slide stays: every 5 s up to every 60 s . Eight to ten seconds suits most notices. Click the first slide to start. A LOOPING label shows while it goes round. It stops when you move on to anything else. Black or Logo pauses it (it says PAUSED ). Press the button again to carry on.","summary":""},{"url":"announcement-loops.html#with-walk-in-music","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"With walk-in music","text":"With walk-in music Start the loop. In the Media tab, open Music and Audio and click ▶ Play all . When the service starts, click Fade out under Music and audio, then click your first song.","summary":""},{"url":"announcement-loops.html#a-countdown-too","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"A countdown too","text":"A countdown too To show “Service starts in 4:59” with the notices, start a Count down to time in Timer and prompts, and tick Also show the countdown on the congregation screen . See Timers and prompts .","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"","text":"Themes: how the words look A theme is how the words look: the font, size, colour and what’s behind them.","summary":"Fonts, sizes, colours, outlines, where the words sit and what goes behind them, for songs, Bible verses and sermons. How slides change."},{"url":"themes.html#the-built-in-themes","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"The built-in themes","text":"The built-in themes Classic : the starting theme. Bold outline : thick letters with an outline, easy to read over busy backgrounds. Elegant : a serif font. High contrast (easy to read) : for people who find the screen hard to read. Words at the bottom : leaves the picture clear above.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#choosing-a-theme-for-songs-bible-verses-and-sermons","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"Choosing a theme for songs, Bible verses and sermons","text":"Choosing a theme for songs, Bible verses and sermons Under each theme, Use for: has Songs , Bible and Sermons . Click to make that theme the one they get. For example, songs in Classic and Bible verses in Elegant.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#one-item-in-a-different-theme","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"One item in a different theme","text":"One item in a different theme Select an item in the service. Above its slides, choose a Theme: from the list. Only that item changes.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#a-video-behind-one-song-communion-for-example","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"A video behind one song (communion, for example)","text":"A video behind one song (communion, for example) Tip: For a worship video with its own music, the easiest way is to make it the song’s backing video : see Sing along with a worship video . A theme is best for a calm, silent video behind several songs. For a reflective moment, put a calm video behind just one song: Add the video in the Media tab (a candle, bread and wine, or a starter background ). Click + New theme , name it (“Communion”), and set Behind the words to A picture or video , choosing the video. In the service, select the song and choose Theme: Communion above its slides. The video loops behind the words for that song only. Sound: a background video is silent unless you tick Play the video’s sound in the theme (it shows when the background is a video). Then the video’s own sound plays through the projector computer’s sound, starting from the beginning each time a song with that theme comes on. That’s useful for a video with its own music, and the words can even move by themselves in time with it . Or keep the video silent and give the song a backing track , or play music from Music and audio. Note: Words can’t go over a YouTube video (YouTube doesn’t allow anything on top of its player). Use a video file you have permission to show.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#changing-a-theme-or-making-your-own","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"Changing a theme, or making your own","text":"Changing a theme, or making your own Click a theme’s picture to change it. Built-in themes can’t be changed: clicking one makes your own copy to change. Or click + New theme . Name . Font , and Largest text size . Long verses get smaller by themselves to fit; this is how big short ones get. Show all fonts on this computer lists every font. Bold , Shadow , ALL CAPITALS . Text colour , Outline (None, Thin, Thick) and its colour. Line up the words (left, centre, right) and Where on the screen (top, middle, bottom). Behind the words : The church background (chosen in the Media tab), A picture or video , A plain colour , or Black . For a video, tick Play the video’s sound to hear it. The Preview shows how song words will look on the projector. Click Save theme . Delete theme removes your own theme; anything using it goes back to Classic. Tip: At the back of the room, bigger and simpler is always easier. White text with a thin outline or shadow on a dark background is easiest of all.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#how-slides-change","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"How slides change","text":"How slides change At the top of the Themes tab, Changing slides : Fade (the usual), Cut (instant), Slide , Zoom , Rise . Speed: Quick , Normal , Slow . This is for every screen and every item.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html","page":"Screens","heading":"","text":"Screens PraiseAV can drive several screens at once, each with its own job.","summary":"Projectors and TVs: which screen shows what, the screen map with tap-to-swap, practice screens for setting up at home, and opening a view in a window."},{"url":"screens.html#the-four-views","page":"Screens","heading":"The four views","text":"The four views View What it shows Usually on Congregation Words, verses, videos, pictures The projector or big TVs at the front Preacher Notes, the band’s words during songs, clock, timer, prompts A TV at the back, facing the platform Timer One big timer and prompts A small screen on the platform Livestream The words in a strip, for a video mixer to lay over the camera A cable to an ATEM or capture card The computer’s own screen is always PraiseAV controls : the window you work in.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#plugging-in-screens","page":"Screens","heading":"Plugging in screens","text":"Plugging in screens Plug the projector or TV into the computer (HDMI, usually). Windows must extend onto it, not copy. If Windows is copying the laptop’s screen onto the projector, PraiseAV shows a yellow message at the top: click Use it as a separate screen . (It’s the same as pressing Windows + P and choosing Extend .) PraiseAV finds it. The first screen gets Congregation , the next Preacher , then Timer . PraiseAV remembers each screen’s job. Next Sunday, everything appears in the same place by itself. With no screens plugged in, the congregation view opens in a window, so you can still try everything.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#choosing-each-screen-s-job","page":"Screens","heading":"Choosing each screen’s job","text":"Choosing each screen’s job Click Screens… at the top right. Under Each screen’s job , each plugged-in screen has a list: Congregation , Preacher , Timer , Livestream , or Not used .","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#the-screen-map","page":"Screens","heading":"The screen map","text":"The screen map At the top of the Screens window, a map shows every screen, live, with what’s on it now. Solid boxes with ● Connected are real screens plugged in. Dashed boxes aren’t on a screen yet: Practice only , or Planned: plug in a screen (from your church setup ).","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#swapping-two-screens","page":"Screens","heading":"Swapping two screens","text":"Swapping two screens The preacher’s notes are on the front projector, and the words on the back TV? Tap one, then tap the other. They swap. Tap a dashed view, then a solid screen, to put that view on it.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#the-preacher-screen","page":"Screens","heading":"The preacher screen","text":"The preacher screen What the preacher’s screen shows, and how big, is up to you: see Sermons and the preacher’s screen . During songs it doubles as the band’s words.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#practice-screens","page":"Screens","heading":"Practice screens","text":"Practice screens Set everything up at home on a laptop, or show a church what the preacher and timer screens do before they’ve bought any. In Screens… , click Open practice screens . One window shows every view side by side, live, as you go through the service.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#a-monitor-for-watching-every-screen","page":"Screens","heading":"A monitor for watching every screen","text":"A monitor for watching every screen Plug in a spare monitor next to the laptop and set its job to Not used . Open practice screens then fills that monitor, so you can watch the front, the back wall, the timer and the livestream while you work. It s handy for demos too: the church s projector shows the real thing, and everyone can see the other screens on the monitor. Each view has a Show on… list, to put it on a real screen when one is plugged in. Views with no screen say Practice only . Tip: At home, plug in a spare TV or monitor as a pretend projector. Set up the screens, save a church setup , and take it to church on a USB stick or OneDrive.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#open-a-view-in-a-window","page":"Screens","heading":"Open a view in a window","text":"Open a view in a window In Screens… , under Open a view in a window , click Congregation , Preacher , Timer or Livestream . It opens in a normal window. This is handy for a screen PraiseAV can’t see by itself (some wireless displays and USB adaptors): drag the window onto it and maximise it.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#hide-and-show","page":"Screens","heading":"Hide and Show","text":"Hide and Show Hide at the top right takes all the screens away (to use the projector for something else). Show brings them back.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#if-a-screen-isn-t-found","page":"Screens","heading":"If a screen isn’t found","text":"If a screen isn’t found Press Windows + P and choose Extend , not Duplicate. Check the projector is on and set to the right input (HDMI 1, HDMI 2…). Unplug the cable and plug it back in. See Troubleshooting .","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"","text":"Live video on the screen PraiseAV can put live video on the congregation screen with the words over it or under it. For example: A camera on the preacher, so people at the back can see (a big room, or an overflow hall). A YouTube live stream from another church, for a joint service. Open Live video in the right-hand column.","summary":"Show a camera, or a YouTube or web stream, on the congregation screen with the words, for overflow rooms, a big room, or a joint service."},{"url":"live-video.html#a-camera","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"A camera","text":"A camera Plug the camera in. Most cameras need a USB capture device to connect to a computer (see Hardware ). Webcams just plug in. Click Capture device , and choose the camera from the list. Not there? Click Refresh . Choose the sound: usually No sound (the sound desk has it) . Choose how the words show: Words in a strip along the bottom : the picture stays clear. Words full screen over a dimmed picture . Tick On (or press V ). Songs and verses show with the video. Videos, pictures and the logo still show full screen when you put them on; the camera comes back after.","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#phone-cameras","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"Phone cameras","text":"Phone cameras Old phones make good cameras. Prop one up where it’s needed, on the church Wi-Fi, and its picture comes into PraiseAV. A few donated phones can be your cameras until there’s money for proper ones. In Live video , click Phone camera , then + Add a phone camera . A QR code appears. On the phone, on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer, scan the code with its camera. The first time, the phone warns that the connection isn’t private . That’s because PraiseAV is on your computer, not a website on the internet. Tap Advanced (or Show details ), then Continue (or visit this website ). Each phone asks once. Give it a name (“Pulpit camera”), tap Start the camera , and allow the camera. Turn the phone sideways, prop it up, and plug it into a charger . The phone appears in the list with ● Connected . Choose it, and tick On . The phone says ● LIVE on screen while PraiseAV is showing it. Add as many phones as you like, and switch between them by choosing a different one in the list. Each phone remembers its name, so next time just open the page again and tap Start the camera . Tip: Phone cameras send a picture over the Wi-Fi about 15 times a second, slightly behind real life. That’s fine for a camera on the screen or a recording , but for lip-sync-perfect livestreams, cameras plugged into a capture card are better. The first time phone cameras are switched on, Windows may ask on the PraiseAV computer whether to allow PraiseAV on private networks : choose Allow .","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#youtube-or-a-web-link","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"YouTube or a web link","text":"YouTube or a web link Click YouTube or web link . Paste the link (for example https://youtube.com/live/… ) and click Use . Tick On . The video sits at the top with the words underneath: YouTube doesn’t allow anything on top of its player. Note: This needs good internet at church. Streams run a few seconds behind.","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#the-logo-in-the-corner","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"The logo in the corner","text":"The logo in the corner Tick Church logo in the corner and choose the corner. This works with or without live video. Choose your logo in the Media tab first, or a different picture just for the corner (see Media ).","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#recording-instead","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"Recording instead","text":"Recording instead No livestream? Record the service : with Live video on, the recording shows the camera with the words.","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#livestreaming-is-different","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"Livestreaming is different","text":"Livestreaming is different This page is about showing video in the room . To send your service out to YouTube or Facebook, see Livestream and ATEM .","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"","text":"Livestream and ATEM When you stream a service, viewers at home want to see the room and read the words. The Livestream screen gives a video mixer just the words, in a strip, ready to lay over the camera.","summary":"Put the song words over your camera on a livestream, with a Blackmagic ATEM Mini or OBS. Start and stop streaming and recording from PraiseAV."},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#how-it-fits-together","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"How it fits together","text":"How it fits together Camera HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 1 PraiseAV computer Livestream screen HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 2 Sound desk a separate mix for the stream 3.5 mm audio ATEM Mini Pro mic input ATEM Mini Pro Ethernet to the router YouTube or Facebook and PraiseAV controls it The ATEM keys the words over the camera: it cuts out the black (or green) and keeps the words. See Example setups for the whole picture.","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#setting-up-the-livestream-screen","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"Setting up the Livestream screen","text":"Setting up the Livestream screen Plug a second HDMI output of the computer into an ATEM input. In Screens… , give that screen the Livestream job. Under Livestream in the same window, choose the Background : Black: for a luma key : the usual for an ATEM. Green: for a chroma key (the words sit on a dark band): for OBS, vMix or mixers that key on green. Optional: your CCLI Streaming Licence number . It shows small under the song details. Only songs and verses show on the Livestream screen. Parent alerts never go on it , so children’s numbers stay private.","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#controlling-an-atem-from-praiseav","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"Controlling an ATEM from PraiseAV","text":"Controlling an ATEM from PraiseAV PraiseAV can talk to a Blackmagic ATEM Mini (any model) over the network. Plug the ATEM into the same network as the computer with an Ethernet cable (USB alone isn’t enough). Click ATEM… at the top of PraiseAV, and tick Control a Blackmagic ATEM switcher from PraiseAV . Type the ATEM’s address and click Connect . A new ATEM Mini uses 192.168.10.240 . Blackmagic’s ATEM Setup app shows it. When it says Connected , choose: Which input is this computer (the slides)? Keyer that puts the livestream words over the camera : usually Downstream key 1 . Or None if you’d rather do it on the ATEM’s buttons. Tick Turn the words on and off by themselves so the words appear when a song or verse is on screen and go away for the sermon. Under Try it : Show slides , Back to camera , Words on , Words off . Check each one works. The window also shows the ATEM’s model and firmware. If the firmware is older than 8.0, update it with ATEM Setup.","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#during-the-service","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"During the service","text":"During the service At the top of PraiseAV: ● Go live starts streaming, ● Record starts recording (on models that can). They show ON AIR and REC with the time. PraiseAV asks before starting or stopping the stream. In the right-hand column, Livestream shows what’s on the stream, with Show slides and Back to camera for full-screen slides (a video, notices). The stream key and YouTube scheduling stay in ATEM Software Control and YouTube Studio : set them up once there. If something isn’t working, Open the ATEM log (bottom of the ATEM window) keeps a record to send to whoever helps you.","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#with-obs-instead","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"With OBS instead","text":"With OBS instead OBS Studio is free streaming software for a computer. Use a capture card to bring the Livestream screen into the OBS computer (or run OBS on the PraiseAV computer and use a Window capture of a Livestream window: Screens… › Open a view in a window › Livestream ). Choose the Green background. In OBS, add a Chroma Key filter to that source and put it above the camera.","summary":""},{"url":"recording.html","page":"Recording the service","heading":"","text":"Recording the service If your church can’t livestream (no internet, or nobody to run it), PraiseAV can record the service to a video file. Upload it to YouTube or Facebook afterwards, or share it with people who couldn’t come. It records what’s on the congregation screen : the slides, or with Live video on, the camera with the words over it.","summary":"No livestream? Record what's on the congregation screen, with the sound desk's sound, to a video file to upload to YouTube or Facebook later."},{"url":"recording.html#recording","page":"Recording the service","heading":"Recording","text":"Recording Click ● Record at the top of PraiseAV. Choose where the sound comes from: The sound desk : the best. Run a lead from the desk into the computer, usually through a small USB audio interface (see Hardware ), and choose it here. This computer’s sound : only PraiseAV’s own videos and music, not the people speaking or singing. A microphone : works, but a laptop’s microphone hears the room and sounds echoey. Click ● Start recording . The button shows ■ REC and the time while it records. Click it to stop. PraiseAV asks first, then saves the file. The file goes in Documents › PraiseAV Recordings , named with the date and the service. Click Show the file in the message to open the folder. Tip: Turn Live video on with a phone camera pointed at the front, and the recording shows the room with the song words along the bottom, like a livestream.","summary":""},{"url":"recording.html#good-to-know","page":"Recording the service","heading":"Good to know","text":"Good to know The recording is an MP4 file at the projector’s size (usually 1920 × 1080), which YouTube and Facebook accept as it is. It’s saved a few seconds at a time, so a power cut loses only the last few seconds. If the congregation screen closes (the projector is unplugged, or Hide is pressed), the recording stops and is saved. The laptop doesn’t sleep while it’s recording. Plug it in: an hour’s recording uses a lot of battery. An hour is about 2.5 GB. Make sure there’s space on the computer. Note: Putting a service online needs the right licences: a CCLI Streaming Licence for the songs, and permission for any videos you show. Recording for your own use needs no streaming licence until you upload it.","summary":""},{"url":"recording.html#with-an-atem-mini","page":"Recording the service","heading":"With an ATEM Mini","text":"With an ATEM Mini If you have an ATEM Mini Pro, it can record too ( ● Record next to Go live ): see Livestream and ATEM .","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"","text":"Phone and tablet remote Anyone you allow can control PraiseAV from a phone, tablet or laptop, in its web browser. Nothing to install, and it works on Android, iPhone, iPad and laptops. An AV remote does what the desk does: Next, Black, Logo, any slide in the service. A preacher remote moves the preacher’s own slides and notes, and shows the timer.","summary":"Control PraiseAV from a phone, tablet or laptop on the church Wi-Fi: AV remote or preacher remote. No app to install."},{"url":"phone-remote.html#switching-it-on","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"Switching it on","text":"Switching it on Click Remote at the top right of PraiseAV. Click Switch remote control on .","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#connecting-a-phone","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"Connecting a phone","text":"Connecting a phone The phone must be on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer. Scan the code with the phone’s camera. Or type the address shown into the phone’s browser, then the PIN . Type a name (“Pastor Sam”) and tap Connect . On the PraiseAV computer, a message asks about it. Click Allow as AV remote , Allow as preacher remote , or No . Nobody can control PraiseAV until you allow them at the desk. The PIN changes each time remote control is switched on. Allowed devices are listed under Allowed devices . Next time, they connect straight away. Remove stops one.","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#the-av-remote","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"The AV remote","text":"The AV remote ◀ Back and Next ▶ , big. Black , Clear text , Logo . What’s on screen now. The Service : tap any item, then any slide. For sermons: ◀ Notes and Notes ▶ .","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#the-preacher-remote","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"The preacher remote","text":"The preacher remote ▶ Start my sermon : puts the sermon on screen, for when there’s no one at the laptop. It shows whenever the sermon isn’t on screen. ◀ Slide and Slide ▶ for the congregation slides (while the sermon is on screen). ◀ Notes and Notes ▶ , with the notes themselves on the phone or laptop. The timer , which turns amber and then red, like the preacher’s screen. A preacher with a laptop on the lectern can use it as a notes screen and a clicker together. No one free for the laptop? See Running a service without an AV volunteer .","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#leading-worship-and-preaching-from-one-phone","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"Leading worship and preaching from one phone","text":"Leading worship and preaching from one phone Someone who leads worship and preaches (a worship leader who is also the youth pastor, say) only needs one phone, allowed once as an AV remote : At the bottom of the AV remote, tap ⇄ Switch to the preacher’s view for the sermon: the notes, the timer, Start my sermon and the slide buttons. Tap ⇄ Switch to the AV view to lead worship again and finish the service. Switch as often as you like. There’s no pairing again, and nobody needs to go to the laptop. A preacher remote can’t switch, so a visiting speaker can’t change the songs. To give someone more, change their phone at the laptop: Remote → Allowed devices , choose AV remote (and preacher’s view) next to their name. There’s no need to pair again. Forget this phone (or Switch to a different remote on a preacher remote) takes the phone off the list; it then has to join again.","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#if-the-laptop-s-battery-is-low","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"If the laptop’s battery is low","text":"If the laptop’s battery is low The remotes show Laptop battery 25%: plug it in at the top when the laptop is running low on battery.","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#phones-can-t-connect","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"Phones can’t connect?","text":"Phones can’t connect? Same network : guest Wi-Fi often keeps devices apart. Use the staff Wi-Fi. The first time , Windows asks on the PraiseAV computer whether to allow PraiseAV on private networks : choose Allow . Wi-Fi with no internet : the phone may switch to mobile data. On Android, tap Stay connected when asked. On iPhone, turn mobile data off while using the remote. No Wi-Fi at all? Turn on the PraiseAV computer’s Mobile hotspot (Windows Settings › Network internet) and join phones to that. Note: The remote only works inside your building’s network. It isn’t on the internet, and PraiseAV doesn’t send anything anywhere.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html","page":"Example setups","heading":"","text":"Example setups Every church is different, but most fit one of these. Start with the simplest that does what you need. You can always add to it later, and practice screens let you try a bigger setup before buying anything. For what to buy, see Hardware . Tip: Count the screens that show different things. Each one needs its own output from the computer. Two screens showing the same thing can share one output with a splitter.","summary":"What a PraiseAV setup looks like in practice: from one laptop and a projector, to back-wall TVs, cameras, overflow rooms and livestreaming with an ATEM Mini or OBS."},{"url":"example-setups.html#1-one-laptop-and-a-projector","page":"Example setups","heading":"1. One laptop and a projector","text":"1. One laptop and a projector The simplest setup, and where most churches start. Laptop PraiseAV controls HDMI Projector or big TV Congregation 3.5 mm to the sound desk Sound desk videos and music You need: a laptop with an HDMI socket, an HDMI cable long enough to reach, and the projector or TV. In PraiseAV: nothing to set up. The projector gets the congregation view by itself. Good for: small churches, house churches, halls, a first try.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#2-add-a-screen-for-the-preacher","page":"Example setups","heading":"2. Add a screen for the preacher","text":"2. Add a screen for the preacher The preacher sees their notes, the next slide, the clock and the timer on a TV at the back of the room. Laptop PraiseAV controls HDMI Projector Congregation USB-C to HDMI adaptor, long HDMI cable TV on the back wall Preacher Wi-Fi Preacher’s phone or laptop preacher remote (optional) You need: a laptop with two video outputs (usually HDMI and USB-C), a USB-C to HDMI adaptor, a TV (40 to 55 inch), and a long HDMI cable or an extender (see Hardware ). In PraiseAV: the second screen gets Preacher by itself. Check in Screens… . Good for: preachers who use notes, worship leaders who want the words without turning round.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#3-two-front-screens-a-back-wall-tv-and-a-timer","page":"Example setups","heading":"3. Two front screens, a back wall TV, and a timer","text":"3. Two front screens, a back wall TV, and a timer A larger room with screens on both sides of the platform. Desktop PC PraiseAV controls HDMI HDMI splitter HDMI splitter HDMI Left projector Congregation HDMI Right projector Congregation Desktop PC PraiseAV controls HDMI over Cat6 extender Back wall TV Preacher HDMI Small screen on the platform floor Timer The two front projectors show the same thing, so a splitter feeds both from one output. Windows sees them as one screen. You need: a desktop PC with a graphics card with three or four outputs , an HDMI splitter (1 in, 2 out), and extenders for the long runs. In PraiseAV: in Screens… , give each output its job: Congregation, Preacher, Timer. Careful: Both projectors on a splitter must handle the same resolution. Set them both to 1920 × 1080.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#4-livestreaming-with-an-atem-mini","page":"Example setups","heading":"4. Livestreaming with an ATEM Mini","text":"4. Livestreaming with an ATEM Mini Viewers at home see the camera with the song words along the bottom. The ATEM Mini Pro streams straight to YouTube or Facebook, without a second computer. PraiseAV computer HDMI Projector Congregation HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 2: Livestream screen Camera 1 wide shot from the back HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 1 Camera 2 the preacher (optional) HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 3 Sound desk aux send, 3.5 mm ATEM Mini Pro mic input ATEM Mini Pro Ethernet Router internet to YouTube PraiseAV computer Ethernet or Wi-Fi to the same router Router You need: an ATEM Mini Pro, one or two cameras with HDMI out, HDMI cables, an Ethernet cable from the ATEM to the router, and an audio cable from the sound desk. In PraiseAV: In Screens… , give the ATEM output the Livestream job, with a Black background. Click ATEM… , connect, choose Downstream key 1 , and tick Turn the words on and off by themselves . On Sunday, press ● Go live at the top of PraiseAV. Full steps: Livestream and ATEM . Important: The stream needs its own sound mix from the sound desk (an aux send). The sound in the room is mostly from the speakers, so a room microphone sounds hollow and echoey online. Ask whoever runs sound to set up an aux with every microphone and instrument.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#5-livestreaming-on-a-budget-with-obs","page":"Example setups","heading":"5. Livestreaming on a budget with OBS","text":"5. Livestreaming on a budget with OBS No ATEM: the free OBS Studio does the mixing on the same computer. Camera HDMI USB capture device USB capture device USB 3 PraiseAV computer runs OBS too Sound desk USB audio interface PraiseAV computer runs OBS too PraiseAV computer runs OBS too HDMI Projector Congregation internet YouTube or Facebook In PraiseAV: Screens… › Open a view in a window › Livestream , with a Green background. In OBS, add a Window capture of that window with a Chroma Key filter, above the camera. You need: a more powerful computer (see Hardware ), a capture device, and an audio interface. Good for: trying a stream before buying an ATEM. A webcam works to start with.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#6-a-camera-on-the-big-screen-and-an-overflow-room","page":"Example setups","heading":"6. A camera on the big screen, and an overflow room","text":"6. A camera on the big screen, and an overflow room In a large room, a camera on the preacher shown on the screens helps people at the back. An overflow hall or café gets the same picture on a TV. Camera on the preacher HDMI USB capture device USB capture device USB 3 PraiseAV computer Live video PraiseAV computer Live video HDMI Projector Congregation: camera with words HDMI splitter, then HDBaseT extender TV in the overflow room same as Congregation In PraiseAV: open Live video , choose Capture device , and Words in a strip along the bottom . See Live video . The overflow room needs the service’s sound too: a feed from the sound desk to a speaker there.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#7-cameras-from-donated-phones-and-a-recording","page":"Example setups","heading":"7. Cameras from donated phones, and a recording","text":"7. Cameras from donated phones, and a recording No money for cameras or a livestream yet? A couple of old phones and the PraiseAV laptop. Old phone on a shelf at the back Wi-Fi PraiseAV computer Phone camera Old phone by the pulpit Wi-Fi PraiseAV computer Phone camera Sound desk USB audio interface PraiseAV computer recording sound PraiseAV computer Phone camera HDMI Projector camera with the words In PraiseAV: add each phone with Live video › Phone camera › + Add a phone camera , then switch between them as needed. Press ● Record to save the service to a video file, and upload it to YouTube or Facebook afterwards. See Live video and Recording the service . You need: old phones (any from the last few years), phone chargers and long cables, small phone tripods or clamps, and good Wi-Fi in the room.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#8-setting-up-at-home","page":"Example setups","heading":"8. Setting up at home","text":"8. Setting up at home The AV volunteer can set everything up at home, with no church equipment. Laptop PraiseAV with practice screens HDMI Spare TV or monitor pretend projector In Screens… , click Open practice screens to see every view side by side. Make the themes, alert groups and logo, and plan which screen does what. Save a Church setup to OneDrive or a USB stick, and open it on the church computer. See Church setup . This is also how to show a church what PraiseAV can do before they’ve bought any screens.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#where-to-put-the-computer","page":"Example setups","heading":"Where to put the computer","text":"Where to put the computer At the back , where the operator can see the screens and the front, and hear clearly. With a clicker or the phone remote , the operator can sit anywhere. Keep cables taped down or under mats where people walk. Plug the computer into the same power as the projector if you can, or use a small UPS (battery backup) so a power blip doesn’t stop the service.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"","text":"Hardware: what to buy You don’t need expensive equipment. Most churches can start with a laptop they already have. This page helps when you’re buying, or when something isn’t working well. Note: Prices are rough guides (UK, 2026) and change often. We don’t sell anything or earn from any of these: the makes named are examples that work well, not the only choices.","summary":"Computers, video outputs, projectors and TVs, cables and HDMI extenders for long runs, clickers, cameras, capture cards and which ATEM Mini to choose."},{"url":"hardware.html#the-computer","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"The computer","text":"The computer PraiseAV runs on Windows 10 or 11 . Fine for words, pictures and video Also livestreaming with OBS on the same computer Processor Intel Core i3 / AMD Ryzen 3, from about 2018 on Intel Core i5 or i7 / AMD Ryzen 5 or 7, from about 2020 on Memory 8 GB 16 GB Storage SSD (not an old spinning hard drive) SSD, with room for recordings Video outputs One per screen showing something different Same An ATEM Mini does the streaming work itself, so with an ATEM the PraiseAV computer only needs the first column.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#how-many-screens-can-it-drive","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"How many screens can it drive?","text":"How many screens can it drive? Count the video outputs. Each screen showing something different needs its own. Laptops usually have one HDMI socket, and often a USB-C or Thunderbolt socket that can also send video (look for a small screen or lightning symbol next to it). With a USB-C to HDMI adaptor (about £15) that’s two screens, plus the laptop’s own. Desktop PCs with a graphics card usually have three or four outputs (HDMI and DisplayPort). DisplayPort to HDMI adaptors are cheap. Mini PCs (the size of a sandwich) often have two or three outputs, and are good for a permanent install. Careful: “USB display adaptors” (DisplayLink) that plug into an ordinary USB socket work for still slides, but video can stutter. Use real video outputs for the congregation screen.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#before-sunday","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Before Sunday","text":"Before Sunday Turn off automatic Windows updates restarting during service times ( Settings › Windows Update › Advanced options › Active hours ). Set the power plan so the screen never goes to sleep while plugged in. Turn off notifications ( Focus , or Do not disturb ).","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#projectors","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Projectors","text":"Projectors Room Brightness Small room, lights can be dimmed 3,000 to 3,500 lumens Normal church with windows 4,000 to 5,000 lumens Large or very bright room 6,000 lumens and up Resolution: 1920 × 1080 (“Full HD” or “1080p”) or 1920 × 1200 (“WUXGA”). Avoid “supports 1080p” projectors that are really lower resolution inside. Look for native 1080p. Laser projectors cost more, but last about 20,000 hours with no lamps to replace and switch on instantly. Lamp projectors need a new lamp (£80 to £200) every 3,000 to 5,000 hours. Short-throw projectors sit close to the screen, so people don’t walk through the beam or cast shadows. Makes that churches use a lot: Epson , BenQ , Optoma , Panasonic .","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#how-big-a-screen","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"How big a screen?","text":"How big a screen? A useful rule: the screen height should be at least the distance to the back row ÷ 6 . A back row 12 metres away needs a picture at least 2 metres high. For a 16:9 picture, that’s about 3.5 metres wide.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#tvs-instead-of-projectors","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"TVs instead of projectors","text":"TVs instead of projectors Big TVs are now cheaper than projectors for smaller rooms, and work with the lights on. 55 to 75 inch 4K TVs are fine; PraiseAV sends a sharp 1080p picture that looks good on them. On the TV, choose Game or PC mode, and turn off motion smoothing . If the edges of the picture are cut off, find Picture size and choose Just scan , Screen fit or 1:1 . For a preacher’s screen on the back wall, a 40 to 55 inch TV is plenty.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#getting-the-picture-there","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Getting the picture there","text":"Getting the picture there HDMI cables are reliable up to about 10 to 15 metres . Further than that, the picture flickers or doesn’t appear. Distance Use Up to 10 m An ordinary HDMI cable (“High Speed” or “Premium High Speed”) 10 to 50 m An active optical HDMI cable (fibre inside). One end is marked Source : that end goes to the computer. Up to 70–100 m, or through walls HDMI over Cat6 extenders (look for HDBaseT ). A box at each end, joined by a network cable. Careful: Avoid wireless HDMI and cheap “HDMI over Ethernet” boxes that compress the picture. They drop out or lag, usually mid-service. HDMI splitter (1 in, 2 or 4 out, about £20 to £40): the same picture to several screens, for example two front projectors. Label both ends of every cable (“Front projector”, “Back TV”). It saves a lot of time.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#midi-pedals-and-stream-deck","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"MIDI pedals and Stream Deck","text":"MIDI pedals and Stream Deck A MIDI foot pedal or a keyboard’s sustain pedal lets a worship leader move the words while playing. Any USB MIDI device works. A Stream Deck (Elgato, about £80 to £150) gives the AV desk big labelled buttons. See MIDI and Stream Deck .","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#clickers","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Clickers","text":"Clickers Any presentation clicker works: forward is Next, back is Previous, and the blank button is Black. Look for: A range of 20 metres or more. A USB receiver (no pairing). Examples: Logitech R400 or R500s , and many cheap unbranded ones (about £15 to £40). The phone remote also works as a clicker.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#sound-from-the-computer","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Sound from the computer","text":"Sound from the computer PraiseAV’s video and music sound comes out of the computer: the headphone socket, or HDMI to the projector. The best way to the sound desk is the headphone socket into a DI box (about £25 to £50), then an XLR cable to the desk. A DI box stops hum and buzz. A small USB audio interface (Behringer UCA202, Focusrite Scarlett Solo) gives better sound and a clean connection.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#cameras","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Cameras","text":"Cameras For a livestream, or a camera on the big screen. Look for: Clean HDMI output (no menus or icons on the HDMI picture). Runs from mains power for 2 hours or more without stopping. Some photo cameras stop after 30 minutes, or overheat. Optical zoom , to film the preacher from the back of the room. Good choices: Camcorders (Sony Handycam, Panasonic HC-V range, about £250 to £500): simple, reliable, good zoom. PTZ cameras (pan-tilt-zoom, about £500 to £1,500): mounted on a wall and moved by remote, with preset positions (pulpit, band, whole room). Webcams are fine for trying out, but can’t zoom in from the back. Old phones , free: PraiseAV can use any phone on the Wi-Fi as a camera, with no app to install. Add a phone tripod or clamp (about £10) and a long charging cable. See Live video .","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#capture-cards","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Capture cards","text":"Capture cards A camera’s HDMI needs a capture device to go into a computer (for Live video , or OBS). Plug it into a USB 3 socket (usually blue), directly, not through a hub. Elgato Cam Link 4K (about £100): widely used and reliable. Cheaper USB HDMI capture sticks (about £15 to £30) work well for 1080p. An ATEM Mini connected by USB also shows up as a webcam.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#which-atem-mini","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Which ATEM Mini?","text":"Which ATEM Mini? Blackmagic’s ATEM Mini switchers are small desks that mix cameras and slides. PraiseAV controls all of them. Model Inputs Streams by itself? Records? About ATEM Mini 4 HDMI No: acts as a webcam for OBS or Zoom No £250 ATEM Mini Pro 4 HDMI Yes , over Ethernet to YouTube or Facebook To a USB drive £400 ATEM Mini Pro ISO 4 HDMI Yes Every input separately, for editing later £600 ATEM Mini Extreme 8 HDMI Yes To USB drives £1,000+ For most churches, the ATEM Mini Pro is the one: plug in the cameras and PraiseAV, and stream without another computer. Plug its Ethernet socket into the church router so PraiseAV can control it. Feed it the sound from the desk (an aux send) through its mic input. Update it with ATEM Setup to version 8.0 or later.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#internet-for-livestreaming","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Internet for livestreaming","text":"Internet for livestreaming An upload speed of at least 10 Mbps for a steady 1080p stream (test it at speedtest.net during the week, then again on a Sunday). Use a cable from the ATEM or streaming computer to the router, not Wi-Fi. If the church Wi-Fi is shared with everyone, ask whoever looks after it to keep the streaming cable separate or give it priority.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#shopping-lists","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Shopping lists","text":"Shopping lists Starting out (setup 1): your laptop, an HDMI cable of the right length, a clicker. About £30 if you already have a projector or TV. With a preacher’s screen (setup 2): add a USB-C to HDMI adaptor, a 50 inch TV with a wall bracket, and an active optical HDMI cable or HDBaseT extender. About £450 to £700. Livestream (setup 4): add an ATEM Mini Pro, a camcorder with clean HDMI, a tripod, HDMI cables, an Ethernet cable and an audio cable from the desk. About £800 to £1,200. See Example setups for how each one fits together.","summary":""},{"url":"church-setup.html","page":"Church setup","heading":"","text":"Church setup The person who looks after AV can set everything up at home, then take it to the church computer in one file. It’s also how two computers (say, the main one and a spare) are kept the same.","summary":"Set PraiseAV up at home and take it to church in one file: which screen does what, themes, alert groups, logo, livestream and ATEM settings."},{"url":"church-setup.html#what-it-includes","page":"Church setup","heading":"What it includes","text":"What it includes Which screen does what, in order (front projector, back wall TV…). Themes, and which theme songs, Bible verses and sermons use. Parent alert groups and wording. The church logo, and the corner and print logos if you chose them. What the preacher’s screen shows, and how big. Livestream and ATEM settings, transitions, Bible options, CCLI licence number. It doesn’t include songs, services or media. Those go with services and backups .","summary":""},{"url":"church-setup.html#saving-a-church-setup","page":"Church setup","heading":"Saving a church setup","text":"Saving a church setup Click Screens… , then Church setup… . Type the church’s name. Under Screens at church, left to right , list the screens the church has: for example Congregation (front screen) , then Preacher (back wall) . + Add a screen adds another. Click Save church setup . It’s saved in the same folder as your services, so if that’s OneDrive or Google Drive (see Services ), the church computer sees it by itself. Tip: At home, open Practice screens to see every screen on your laptop while you set things up. Screens not plugged in yet show as Planned on the screen map .","summary":""},{"url":"church-setup.html#the-demo-church","page":"Church setup","heading":"The demo church","text":"The demo church For showing PraiseAV to another church, or practising: in Church setup… , click Load the demo church . In a few seconds PraiseAV is set up as Grace Church , with: a logo (with a print version and a symbol for the corner) the free starter backgrounds , with Soft Light behind the words walk-in music, and parent alert groups (Crèche, Sunday School, Youth) a whole service: a welcome slide, Amazing Grace (with chords for the band), Psalm 23, a sermon with notes, two more hymns, and notices that go round by themselves Your own setup is saved first under your church’s name in Church setup , so you can open it again afterwards. Songs and saved services aren’t changed. Everything in the demo is free to use: the hymns are public domain, the Bible reading is the BSB, and PraiseAV makes the logo, backgrounds and music itself.","summary":""},{"url":"church-setup.html#using-it-on-the-church-computer","page":"Church setup","heading":"Using it on the church computer","text":"Using it on the church computer Click Screens… , then Church setup… . Under Use a church setup , click the church’s setup. Or Open from somewhere else (USB stick)… . PraiseAV asks first, because it replaces this computer’s themes, alert groups, logo and screen jobs. Songs, services and media aren’t changed. When the screens are plugged in, each gets its job in the order you listed. You can still change any of them in Screens… .","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"","text":"Backups and moving to a new computer","summary":"Back up all your songs, settings and services to one file, automatic weekly backups, and moving PraiseAV to a new computer."},{"url":"backups.html#backing-up","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Backing up","text":"Backing up Click Open (in the Service column), then Backup and moving… . Tick Include media if you want your pictures, videos and music in it too. It shows how big they are. Click Back up now… and choose where. A USB stick or OneDrive is best: somewhere that isn’t this computer. The backup is one file ( .praiseav-backup ) with all your songs, settings (themes, alert groups, logo…), CCLI song usage and saved services.","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html#automatic-backups","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Automatic backups","text":"Automatic backups Once a week, PraiseAV backs up your songs, settings and services by itself (not media) to Documents › PraiseAV Backups . It keeps the last 8. The Backup window says when the last one was. Tip: If your Documents folder is in OneDrive (it often is on new computers), these backups are off the computer already.","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html#moving-to-a-new-computer","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Moving to a new computer","text":"Moving to a new computer On the old computer, back up with media . Install PraiseAV on the new computer. Click Open , Backup and moving… , then Choose a backup… . It says what’s in it: which computer, when, how many songs and services. Click Bring it in .","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html#bringing-in-a-backup-is-safe","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Bringing in a backup is safe","text":"Bringing in a backup is safe Nothing on this computer is deleted. Songs you already have are skipped, and files already here are kept. So you can bring in a backup from another computer to add its songs without losing your own. The church’s settings (themes, logo…) are taken from the backup.","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html#where-praiseav-keeps-things","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Where PraiseAV keeps things","text":"Where PraiseAV keeps things What Where Media Documents › PraiseAV Media (or the folder you chose) Automatic backups Documents › PraiseAV Backups Services Wherever you chose in Open (this computer, OneDrive, Google Drive…) Songs and settings Inside PraiseAV. Use a backup to copy them.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"","text":"Troubleshooting Important: In the middle of a service, press Black ( B ). It always works, and gives you a moment to sort things out.","summary":"What to do when the projector shows nothing, the wrong screen shows the words, a video won't play, there's no sound, or a phone or ATEM won't connect."},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-projector-shows-nothing","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The projector shows nothing","text":"The projector shows nothing Is Black or Logo on at the top? Press it again. Is the projector on, and on the right input (HDMI 1, HDMI 2…)? Use its remote. Press Windows + P and choose Extend . In PraiseAV, click Hide then Show at the top right. Open Screens… . Is the projector listed, with the Congregation job? Unplug the HDMI cable at both ends and plug it back in.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-projector-shows-my-desktop-or-a-copy-of-my-screen","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The projector shows my desktop, or a copy of my screen","text":"The projector shows my desktop, or a copy of my screen Windows is copying (duplicating) your screen onto the projector. PraiseAV notices this and shows a yellow message at the top: click Use it as a separate screen . Or press Windows + P and choose Extend . PraiseAV then finds the projector and puts the words on it.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-words-are-on-the-wrong-screen","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The words are on the wrong screen","text":"The words are on the wrong screen Open Screens… . On the screen map , tap the two screens to swap them. PraiseAV remembers this for next time.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-video-won-t-play","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A video won’t play","text":"A video won’t play PraiseAV says if a file won’t play. Some formats (WMV, AVI, ProRes) can’t be read. Convert the video to MP4 with the free HandBrake app (choose the “Fast 1080p30” preset) and add it again. If a video stutters, it may be 4K or very large. A 1080p MP4 plays smoothly on almost any computer.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#no-sound","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"No sound","text":"No sound Click the speaker icon by the clock (bottom right of Windows) and check the right output is chosen and not muted. Check the Volume under Music and audio. Check the sound desk channel is up. Videos play their sound from the congregation screen’s window; music from Music and audio. Both use the Windows output.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-words-are-too-small-or-too-big","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The words are too small or too big","text":"The words are too small or too big Go to the Themes tab, click the theme, and change Largest text size . See Themes . Long verses shrink by themselves to fit.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-powerpoint-won-t-come-in","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A PowerPoint won’t come in","text":"A PowerPoint won’t come in PraiseAV needs Microsoft PowerPoint or the free LibreOffice to open PowerPoint files. Install LibreOffice, or ask for a PDF. See PowerPoint and PDF .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-camera-isn-t-in-the-list","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The camera isn’t in the list","text":"The camera isn’t in the list Click Refresh in Live video. Close any other program using the camera (Zoom, Teams, OBS, the Camera app). Plug the capture device into a blue USB 3 socket, directly, not through a hub. See Live video .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-phone-can-t-connect-to-the-remote","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A phone can’t connect to the remote","text":"A phone can’t connect to the remote It must be on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer (not guest Wi-Fi), and Windows must allow PraiseAV on private networks. See Phone remote .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-phone-camera-won-t-connect","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A phone camera won’t connect","text":"A phone camera won’t connect The phone must be on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer (not guest Wi-Fi). If the phone says the connection isn’t private, tap Advanced (or Show details ) and Continue . That’s expected. If it says the camera wasn’t allowed, allow the camera for the page in the browser’s settings. If the computer’s Wi-Fi address changed, scan the new code in Live video › Phone camera › + Add a phone camera . See Live video .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-recording-has-no-sound","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A recording has no sound","text":"A recording has no sound In ● Record , check Sound from is the sound desk’s input, and that the desk is sending sound to it. This computer’s sound only records PraiseAV’s own videos and music. See Recording the service .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-atem-won-t-connect","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The ATEM won’t connect","text":"The ATEM won’t connect The ATEM must be plugged into the network with an Ethernet cable , the same network as the computer. Check its address in Blackmagic’s ATEM Setup app (with the ATEM plugged in by USB). A new ATEM Mini uses 192.168.10.240 . If your router gives addresses like 192.168.1.x , change the ATEM’s address in ATEM Setup to one in that range (for example 192.168.1.240 ). See Livestream and ATEM .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-laptop-went-to-sleep-or-the-projector-went-blank","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The laptop went to sleep, or the projector went blank","text":"The laptop went to sleep, or the projector went blank While PraiseAV is showing the screens, or the phone remote is on, it keeps the laptop awake. If it still sleeps: Is PraiseAV’s Hide on (top right)? Click Show . Check the laptop is plugged in: with a very low battery, Windows may sleep anyway. PraiseAV shows the battery at the top when it’s unplugged. Closing the laptop’s lid usually sends it to sleep. Leave it open, or in Windows Settings › System › Power › Lid close action , choose Do nothing when plugged in.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-power-went-off-or-praiseav-closed","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The power went off, or PraiseAV closed","text":"The power went off, or PraiseAV closed Open PraiseAV again. Your current service is still there: it’s kept as you work, all the time.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-song-or-service-is-missing","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A song or service is missing","text":"A song or service is missing Saved services: click Open . Check Where services are kept : are they in OneDrive on the other computer? Songs: search in the Songs tab by a line of the words, not just the title. Bring them back from a backup: Open › Backup and moving… › Choose a backup… . Nothing you have is deleted. See Backups .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#still-stuck","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"Still stuck?","text":"Still stuck? Ask through praiseav.com . Say what you were doing, what you expected, and what happened. A photo of the screen helps.","summary":""}];
